    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Multiple Sclerosis is a neurological disorder characterized by demyelinated lesions in the central nervous system (CNS), inflammation mediated largely by autoimmune T cells, and eventual motor and sensory loss. Remyelination in the disease is often slow or incomplete, and mechanisms of remyelination are still largely undescribed.  Normal myelination occurs through the differentiation of oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) into functionally mature oligodendrocytes capable of wrapping myelin onto axons.  Electrical activation in a premotor neural circuit via optogenetic stimulation induces proliferation of OPCs, oligodendrogenesis, and increased myelination of that circuit. This mechanism could potentially be used as a tool to enhance remyelination after injury. In this work, we determine whether optogenetic stimulation can enhance remyelination in the motor cortex. We show that optogenetic stimulation increases proliferation of OPCs in the corpus callosum following lysolecithin-induced demyelination of that region.  Since OPCs are the precursors to mature oligodendrocyts and myelin formation, optogenetic stimulation may provide the first trigger to remyelination.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
